= The Casas Viejas Massacre

In January 1933, a new wave of anarchist insurrections stroke Spain. In most places the security forces were able to crush them without any problems. However, in the small village of *Casas Viejas* (Cádiz). Strikers affiliated with the CNT declare libertarian communism and besiege the local Guardia Civil post killing a sargeant and a guard. 

The government dispatches squads of Guardia Civil [? if assault_guard_formed = 0 : and Guardia de Asalto ? ] to restore order. 72 years old Francisco Cruz Gutiérrez "Seisdedos", was found responsible of the assault and when the officers tried to break into his house, the anarchists opened fire, killing one guard and wounding another at the entrance.  Under high tension and being ordered to have no mercy against the shooter, Captain Manuel Rojas orders opening fire with machine guns and the burning of the anarchist cottage, a man and a woman were shot as they tried to leave the fire and other six burned inside. Only a woman carrying a child was spared. 

That same day, Rojas detained various suspects and ordered the summary execution of 12 captured peasants. Twenty-two villagers and three guards were killed that day.

The tragedy detonates a political earthquake. The right-wing press and anarchists accuse Manuel Azaña of ordering a bloodbath (*" neither wounded nor prisoners, shoot to the belly"*). Azaña’s moral authority is critically wounded.

Taking advantage of the government’s crisis, monarchists Antonio Goicoechea and José Calvo Sotelo form a new party, *Renovación Española* (RE). With support from king Alfonso XIII and links to the *Comunión Carlista*, takes inspirtation from the french *Action française*. Unless the somewaht pragmatic or accidentalist Calvo Sotelo's *Acción Popular*, they openly reject the republic and the democracy. 

- @defend_azana: Stand firmly by Azaña and defend the Guardia's institutional integrity.
- @condemn_excesses: Condemn police brutality publicly to protect the PSOE’s standing among workers.
- @condemn_azaña: Condemn police brutality publicly and demand Azaña to be removed from the Cabinet for his responsability.

@defend_azana
subtitle: Blame the tragedy on anarchist provocateurs and maintain coalition loyalty.
on-arrival: workers_psoe -= 10; caballeristas_dissent += 10; ar_relation += 10; rural_psoe -= 20; smallholders_psoe -= 10; middle_psoe -= 5; 
view-if: psoe_in_government == 1

We maintain our cabinet posts and defend Azaña against parliamentary attacks. However, millions of workers view our stance as complicity in state violence, driving radicalized laborers directly into the arms of the CNT and FAI.

@condemn_excesses
subtitle: Condemn the brutal actions of Captain Rojas.
on-arrival: workers_psoe -= 5; caballeristas_dissent += 5; ar_relation += 5; rural_psoe -= 10; smallholders_psoe -= 5; middle_psoe -= 2;
view-if: psoe_in_government == 1

Largo Caballero and UGT leaders publicly condemn the police executions in Casas Viejas, demanding justice for the murdered peasants. While this preserves partly our credibility with the working class, it mortally weakens Azaña's governing coalition from within.

@condemn_azaña
subtitle: Condemn the brutal actions of Captain Rojas and lay the blame on [+ chancellor +].
view-if: psoe_in_government == 0
